\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel0\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs22\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc13278943}Golf Solitaire{\*\bkmkend _Toc13278943}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Golf solitaire is played with a regular deck of cards dealt to seven piles with 5 cards face 
up in each pile. The total number of cards dealt is 35 leaving 17 cards in the stock. The object of the game is to move all of the cards from the tableau piles to the discard pile. Cards can only be moved one at a time and must be in ascending or descendi
n
g sequence with the top-most card of the discard pile ( regardless of suit ). The cards are valued Ace low to King high. The sequence does not end at Ace and does not wrap to King. Therefore, it is only legal to move a '2' on to an Ace. Also, an Ace can b
e moved on to a '2'. A King can be played on a Queen but, the sequence ends there -- meaning a Queen cannot be played on a King. 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1 
\par As a program option, you can allow a Queen to be moved to a King by checking the checkbox,'Options : Customize' : 'Allow Queen on King' . 
\par 
\par Play begins by either tapping the stock to reveal the first card in the deck or any of the top most cards in the tableau piles to move that card to the discard pile. The object of the game is to move all of the cards from the tableau piles to
 the discard pile. Cards can only be moved one at a time and must be in ascending or descending sequence with the top-most card of the discard pile ( regardless of suit ). The cards are valued Ace low to King high. The sequence does not end at Ace and doe
s not wrap to King. Therefore, it is only legal to move a '2' on to an Ace. Also, an Ace can be moved on to a '2'. A King can be played on a Queen but, the sequence ends there -- meaning a Queen cannot be played on a King. 
\par 
\par Try to move as many cards from t
he tableau piles in ascending and descending sequence to the discard pile before dealing the next card. Your score is the number of cards left in the tableau piles after running through the hand. As with golf, the lower your score the better. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs20\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc13278944}Hearts{\*\bkmkend _Toc13278944}
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1 Hear
ts is played with a standard deck of 52 cards. Cards are valued Ace high down to 2 low. Each heart is worth 1 point and the queen of spades is worth 13. Optionally, the Jack of Diamonds can be scored as negative 10 points. The object of the game is to avo
id scoring points and the game is usually over when one player reaches 100 points. If there is a tie for the least number of points, play continues until there is a clear winner. 
\par 
\par Typically, the player with the 2 of Clubs leads the first trick of each hand
. However, as a game option, you can select to always have the player left of the dealer start the first trick. The first dealer of each game will always be the player across from your hand. The deal is then passed clockwise to the next player after each 
hand. 
\par After the first card of a trick is played, play continues clockwise. Each player must follow suit with the lead card whenever possible. If the player cannot follow suit, they can then play a point card or other suit. Once a heart is played on a trick
, hearts are said to be 'broken' and can then be lead in the following tricks. The highest value card that followed suit wins the trick and any points are scored to that player. The top-left corner of your screen displays the score for the current hand. '
A' is the player Across from you; 'R' is the player to your right; 'L' is the player to your left; and 'Y' is You. The winner of the trick then leads the next trick. Play continues until all 13 tricks are played. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs20\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc13278945}Klondike{\*\bkmkend _Toc13278945}
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1 Klondike solitaire is played with
 a regular deck of cards dealt to seven piles increasing in number from one to seven with only the top cards facing up. The total number of cards dealt is 28 leaving 24 cards in the stock. The object of the game is to move all of the cards to the foundati
o
ns. There is one foundation for each suit and they must be placed in ascending suit sequence from Ace to King. Only the top exposed card of the tableau and discard piles may be moved to the foundations one card at a time. You can move a card to the founda
tion by double tapping it or tap-dragging it to the foundation. 
\par 
\par To play, build on the exposed cards of the tableau piles down in value. You may begin play by either alternating colored suits or building the piles down in the same suit. Building down in th
e same suit is harder. Whichever method you begin your game with will hold for the remainder of the game. Whenever the exposed cards of a pile are cleared, the next card in the pile flips up and is available for play. If a pile is completely cleared, it c
a
n then only be played on with a King. Cards can be moved one at a time or by multiple selection between the tableau piles. To play from the discard pile, tap the stock to expose the next three cards. The top most exposed card in the discard pile is availa
ble for play on the foundations or tableau piles. After all cards are dealt to the discard pile, tap the stock area to replenish the stock. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs20\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc13278946}Spades{\*\bkmkend _Toc13278946}
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1 
Spades is played with a standard deck of 52 cards. Cards are valued Ace high down to 2 low and Spades are co
nsidered a trump suit. Spades can be played either as individuals or with partners. When partner play is selected, your partner is the player across from you and your team is 'We'. Your opponents are left and right of your hand and they are 'They'. When p
layed as individuals, you can select to play with 2 or 3 opponents. 
\par The object of Spades is to win points which are earned by meeting you or your team's bid placed at the beginning of each hand. You can also lose points by falling short of your bids or by 
taking too many tricks beyond your bid. The game is typically over when a team or individual reaches 500 points.
\par 
\par Before play begins, you are asked to enter a bid. This bid is the number of tricks you think you can win with your hand. When playing with par
tners, your bid is combined with 'Across' to make up your team's bid. If your team wins the number of tricks bid, you win 10 points times your bid plus 1 point for each overtrick -- or 'bag'. For every 10 bags accumulated, your team loses 100 points. Ther
efore, it is important to try and meet your bid as closely as possible. 
\par Entering a bid of 'nil' indicates that you are not going to win any tricks. If you are successful with a nil bid, your team earns 50 points. If you fail and win 1 or more tricks, your 
team loses 50 points. When bidding blind nil, the point value is 100. Blind nil is available if your team has a score less than or equal to -100 points. 
\par The eldest hand ( player left of the dealer ) leads the first trick of each hand. The first dealer of 
a game is 'Across' and the deal is passed clockwise before each hand. When playing as individuals, you may also set the game such that the player with the 2 of clubs leads each hand. 
\par After the first card of a trick is played, play continues clockwise. Eac
h player must follow suit with the lead card whenever possible. If the player cannot follow suit, they can then play any suit. Once a spade is played on a trick, spades are said to be 'broken' and can then be lead in the following tricks. If a spade is pl
ayed on a trick, the highest spade card takes the trick since spades are the trump suit. Otherwise, the highest card following suit is the winner.
\par 
\par If your combined bid is met or exceeded, your score is 10 times your bid plus one point for every trick taken
 above your bid. Overtricks are also called 'bags' and 100 points will be deducted from your score each time your team reaches 10 bags. If your combined bid is not met, your score for this hand is -10 times your bid. 
\par If you or your partner bids nil, any o
vertricks from that player will not be counted toward fulfilling your team's bid. That is if you bid 0 and take 1, your partner bids 3 and takes 2, your overtrick will not be counted to fulfill the combined contract. Successfully completing a nil bid give
s your team 50 pts and failing a nil bid results in -50 pts. Blind nil doubles the point value. Blind nils can only be entered once your team's score is less than or equal to -100 points ( or other value defined in the rule options ). 
\par Once a team reaches the game ending score ( default is 500 pts), that team is declared the winner. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs20\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc13278947}Spider{\*\bkmkend _Toc13278947}
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1 
Spider solitaire is played with 2 decks of cards dealt into 10 piles. The first 4 piles have 5 cards down and the last 6 have 4 down. Each pile is then dealt 1 card face up making a total of 54 cards dealt. The remaining 50 cards are left in the stock. 

\par The object of the game is to get 13 cards of a suit on top of a pile from King down to Ace. Each time a suit is assembled, it will be removed from the pile. The game is wo
n if you get all 8 suits assembled. The tableau piles can be built down without regard to suit. However, only same suit sequences can be moved (optionally, mixed suits can be moved). A good strategy is to first make all of your same suit moves before mixi
n
g suits and to free up the face down cards from the piles as soon as possible. When a pile is cleared, you must occupy that space with a card before dealing from the stock. When no more moves are possible, tap the stock to deal 10 more cards face up. Play
 continues until all cards are dealt and all possible moves are made. 
\par 
\par To play the game, tap and drag cards from pile to pile, building down in the same suit from King to Ace. When a suit is fully assembled ( King to Ace ), those cards will be removed from
 the game. The object of the game is to remove all of the cards from the tableau piles by assembling all 8 sets of cards. 
\par A good strategy is to first make all of your same suit moves before mixing suits and to free up the face down cards from the piles as
 soon as possible. When a pile is cleared, you must occupy that space with a card of any value before dealing from the stock. When no more moves are possible, tap the stock to deal 10 more cards face up. Play continues until all cards are dealt and all po
ssible moves are made. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs20\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc13278948}War!{\*\bkmkend _Toc13278948}
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1 
War is a simple game of chance in which a regular 52 card deck is divided in half between 2 players. Each player turns up a card and the player with the highest value card takes both face up cards and places them on the bottom 
of their deck. When both cards are the same value, this is WAR! The players then place 3 more cards face down and the 4th up. The player with the highest face up card takes all 10. If the face up cards are the same value ( draw ), each player places 1 car
d down and another up. This play continues until the face up cards do not have the same value. The player with the highest value card takes all of the cards placed during the war. 
\par Depending on the options set, play continues until either all cards are won or a preset number of wars have been won. 
\par Other options include the number of cards placed down during a war and draw. These options can be set from the 'Options : Customize' dialog.
\par 
\par After the computer turns up the top card from its deck, tap your deck t
o turn up your top card. The player with the highest value card takes the hand and the cards are placed on the bottom of the winner's deck. If the top cards from each deck have the same value, this is WAR! The computer will then turn 3 cards face down and
 
the 4th up. You will then need to tap your deck to deal 3 of your cards down and the 4th up. The hand with the highest face up card wins all 10 cards, which are placed on the bottom of the winner's deck. If the war results in a draw, the computer will the
n place one more card down and a second card up. You will then need to tap your deck twice to deal one card down and the second card up. This play continues until one hand is a winner. 
\par Play continues until one player wins all of the cards or 3 wars. From the menu, select 'Options : Customize' to define how many wars need to be won or if all cards need to be won to win the game and how many cards are dealt down during war and draw. 

\par To change the characters, tap within the character's rectangle. 
\par To change the card style, tap the computer's deck. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel0\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs22\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc364414991}
